course: biology
lesson: 121
title: Spike and Tom: real dogs and cats, enemies or friends?
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In Tom and Jerry, Spike the bulldog chases Tom up trees and guards his puppy Tyke, and it looks as if cats and dogs are born enemies.
explain: Real dogs and cats are not enemies by nature. Many share a sofa, and some curl up asleep on the same rug, especially if they met when young.
explain: Trouble comes from body language: a dog wags its tail to say «hello, let's play», while a cat swishes its tail when it is cross, so each can misread the other.
explain: A dog chases what runs because it comes from wolves that hunt by chasing, and a cat escapes up a tree because its curved claws hook into bark and a dog's blunt claws cannot.
explain: Growls, puffed fur and arched backs are warnings that help animals avoid a real fight, and animals that live together soon settle into house rules, just as Tom and Spike make a deal.
fact: Dogs and cats raised together, or introduced slowly, can become real friends; the idea that they always fight is not true.
fact: A study found that cats and dogs often get on best when the cat comes into the home before the dog.
fact: A dog's loose wag often means friendly excitement, but a cat's swishing tail often means it is annoyed or alert; a friendly cat holds its tail straight up with a little hook at the tip.
fact: A frightened cat arches its back, stands sideways and fluffs out its fur to look bigger; tiny muscles at the hair roots tighten, as when you get goosebumps.
fact: Many dogs chase things that run away, such as cats, squirrels and balls; this is called prey drive, and it comes from their wolf ancestors; a cat that stands still and calm often leaves a dog unsure what to do.
fact: Real dogs can be very protective of their puppies and home, but among pet dogs the mother does most of the puppy care, not the father as Spike does.
fact: Bulldogs were bred by people to have short noses and flat faces; very flat-faced dogs often struggle to breathe, snore and overheat easily.
fact: A dog's nose has hundreds of millions of scent cells, while yours has only a few million, so a dog can follow a trail you cannot even notice.
fact: Some dogs bury bones and toys, as wild dogs, wolves and foxes hide spare food to come back to later, and they remember many of their hiding places.
fact: Cats climb up trees easily but find coming down hard, because all their claws point the wrong way for going down head-first.
fact: A growl is a warning that means «stop» before a bite, so trainers say a dog should never be punished for growling.
fact: Animals living together settle into rules about who sleeps where and who eats first, so real fights become rare.
exercise: Spike the bulldog guards his puppy Tyke. In real pet dogs, who does most of the puppy care? | The mother | The father dog | The oldest puppy | Nobody, puppies look after themselves
exercise: Can a real dog and cat become friends who sleep on the same rug? | Yes, especially if they meet young or are introduced slowly | No, cats and dogs are born enemies and will always fight in the end | Only if the dog is smaller than the cat | Only if they are both female
exercise: A study found cats and dogs often get on best when... | the cat came into the home before the dog | the dog came into the home before the cat | they both arrived on the same day | they are fed from the same bowl
exercise: A dog wags its tail loosely and a cat swishes its tail back. What is each one most likely saying? | The dog is friendly and excited; the cat is annoyed or alert | Both are friendly and happy, and both want to come and play with you | Both are angry and ready to fight | The dog is scared of something; the cat is hungry and wants its food
exercise: How does a relaxed, friendly cat usually hold its tail? | Straight up, hooked at the tip | Swishing hard from side to side all the time | Tucked tightly between its legs | Puffed up big like a bottle brush
exercise: Why does a frightened cat arch its back and puff up its fur? | To look bigger so the threat thinks twice | To keep warm in a sudden chill on a cold night | To shake off fleas | To show it wants to play
exercise: What makes a cat's hairs stand up when it is scared? | Tiny muscles pull the hairs up | The hairs fill up with air like little balloons | Its skin swells up with water inside | Static electricity from the dog
exercise: Why do many dogs chase a cat that bolts across the park? | Running away triggers the chasing urge they got from wolves | Dogs hate the smell of cats | Dogs think cats are lost and want to help them back home again | Dogs are jealous of cats
exercise: Why does Spike have a flat face and a short nose? | People bred bulldogs for that look over many generations | Bulldogs broke their noses fighting cats, and their puppies were born that way | Flat faces help dogs smell better | All wild dogs have flat faces
exercise: Why can a dog follow a trail that no person can see? | Its nose has hundreds of millions of scent cells | It can see footprints glowing in the dark, like a cat can | It hears the footsteps left behind | It tastes the ground with its tongue
exercise: Why do some dogs bury bones in the garden? | Their wild relatives hide spare food to come back to later | To sharpen their claws on the soil | To grow new bones in the ground, like planting seeds in spring | Because the bone tastes better muddy
exercise: Why can a cat escape a dog up a tree when the dog cannot follow? | Cat claws are curved and hook into bark; dog claws are blunt | Dogs are afraid of heights, so they will not even try to jump up | Cats are lighter than any dog | Trees smell bad to dogs
exercise: Why do cats find it hard to come down a tree? | Their claws point the wrong way for going down head-first | Their back legs are too short to reach the branch below them | They are scared of the ground | Their tails get stuck in the branches
exercise: A dog growls and shows its teeth over its food bowl. What is it saying? | Stop, before I bite | Please give me more food | I am happy to share my food | I want to play chase
exercise: Why should a dog never be punished for growling? | It may learn to bite without warning | Growling is good for its throat | It will growl louder next time | Growling means it is ill
exercise: Tom and Spike make a deal to stop fighting. What do real animals living together do? | Settle into rules about who sleeps where and eats first | They fight every single day forever, until one of them leaves home | Never go near each other at all | Swap food bowls every morning
test: «cats n dogs hate each other, it's science innit» What is right? | Not true; many live together as friends, especially if they meet young | True, they are born enemies, and no cat and dog can ever be friends | True, but only bulldogs | True, unless the cat is a kitten
test: «my dog wagged at the cat and it whacked him?? he was being nice» What happened? | A cat's swishing tail can mean cross, so they misread each other | The cat hates all dogs, whatever they do, and always hits them | The dog was really angry | The cat wanted to play
test: In a cartoon, Spike flattens Tom and Tom pops back into shape. What would happen to a real cat? | It would be badly hurt and need a vet | It would spring back just like Tom | It would get bigger next time | Nothing, cats have nine lives
test: «3 dogs and 2 cats on the rug, how many tails is that» How many? | 5 | 4 | 10 | 20
test: «Tyke + Spike + Tom, how many legs between 'em, quick» How many? | 12 | 8 | 10 | 16
test: «dog went mental chasing next door's cat. is he a bad dog?» What is right? | No, chasing what runs is prey drive from his wolf ancestors | Yes, only bad dogs chase | Yes, he wants to hurt cats for fun because he is a mean dog | No, he was just cold
test: What would happen if a cat that is chased stands still and stays calm? | Many dogs stop and seem unsure what to do | The dog chases even faster and catches it at once | The cat's fur falls out | The dog falls asleep at once
test: «bulldog's snoring like a tractor again lol» Why might a bulldog snore so much? | Its flat face makes breathing hard | It is dreaming about chasing next door's cat | Snoring keeps other dogs away from it | It is far too hot from its thick fur
test: «cat's been up the tree an hour, it'll get down by itself, it's a cat duh» What is right? | Cats climb up easily but often struggle to come down head-first | Cats always jump down safely | Cats can stay up forever without food | Cats can walk straight down head-first like squirrels, no problem
test: «shut up and stop growling, Rex!» Why is it wiser to leave a growling dog alone? | The growl is a useful warning before a bite | Growling means the dog is laughing | Growling means it wants a hug | It will stop being hungry
test: A cartoon dog buries a bone and forgets where. What do real foxes and wild dogs often do? | Remember many of their hiding places | Never come back for buried food | Bury only stones, not food | Bury food only in winter
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the relations between domestic dogs and cats. | Animals of the two species which are raised together, or introduced gradually, may form durable friendships | The two species are by nature incapable of peaceful cohabitation under any circumstances | Peaceful cohabitation is possible solely where the dog is smaller than the cat | Peaceful cohabitation is possible solely between animals of the same sex
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a cat that is pursued by a dog customarily ascends a tree. | The curved claws of the cat engage the bark, whereas the blunt claws of the dog do not | Dogs are universally afraid of elevated positions | The scent of trees is intolerable to the dog | The cat is thereby enabled to locate its own food, which it previously concealed among the branches
exam: Ascertain the significance of the growl emitted by a dog in respect of its food bowl. | It constitutes a warning to desist, issued prior to any bite | It constitutes a request for an additional portion | It constitutes an invitation to share the contents | It constitutes an indication of illness
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the breeding of the bulldog? | Its flattened face was selected by human breeders and is frequently associated with difficulty in breathing | Its flattened face is an adaptation acquired in the wild by its wolf ancestors for fighting large prey | Its flattened face confers a superior sense of smell | Its flattened face is shared by all wild members of the dog family
